Hands-On Continuous Deployment Project with ArgoCD 

I recently completed a hands-on project where I installed and configured ArgoCD to deploy applications on a K3s Kubernetes cluster running on an AWS EC2 instance and it was a game-changer to my understanding of continuous deployments.

This project increased my understanding of moving from a GitHub repo to live deployment — using ArgoCD to manage, monitor, and apply changes automatically.

I finally got to see how infrastructure and deployment tools come together in real-world workflows.

Here’s what I worked on:
- Launched 3 EC2 instances: argocd, dev, and prod to simulate real world setups.

- Installed K3s Kubernetes on all 3, and used the argocd instance as both the ArgoCD server and the deployment cluster

- Installed ArgoCD and exposed it using a NodePort service for browser access

- Accessed the ArgoCD UI using the external IP and port

- Created a private GitHub repository for my Kubernetes deployment file.

- Connected GitHub to ArgoCD and deployed the application to a dedicated namespace in my Kubernetes cluster via the ArgoCD dashboard.

- Tested Continuous deployment by changing the number of replicas in the deployment file and watched as ArgoCD performed the update immediately.

✅ Key Takeaways:
- Understood how to expose services like ArgoCD via NodePort for public access

- Practiced configuring and using kubeconfig for managing access and interaction with the Kubernetes cluster.

- Connected ArgoCD to a GitHub repo securely using a personal access token

- Learned how to deploy an app to a namespace from ArgoCD using GitHub as the source

